<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
    ============================================================================
    CardDemo Migration  |  Test-Time Logback Configuration
    File: src/test/resources/logback-test.xml
    Source of truth: AAP §0.5.4 (Test Configuration Updates) and §0.10.5
                     (Security Constraints, NON-NEGOTIABLE).
    ============================================================================

    PURPOSE
    Activated automatically when tests run. Logback's discovery order is
    logback-test.xml, then logback.xml, then logback.groovy; Spring Boot's
    LogbackLoggingSystem honours the same precedence via
    `getStandardConfigLocations()`. Because the file name carries the
    `-test` suffix, the production runtime is unaffected by this file;
    only test runs use it (mvn test, mvn verify, IDE test execution).

    DEFENCE-IN-DEPTH PCI/PII REDACTION (AAP §0.10.5, NON-NEGOTIABLE)
    The user directive "No financial data written to logs at any level"
    is enforced by THREE coordinated controls:

      1. THIS FILE: the CONSOLE appender's pattern layout chains three
         %replace conversion words that mask any substring resembling a
         card number (PAN), an 11-digit account ID, or a labelled balance
         /amount value. Even if production code or a test fixture
         accidentally calls log.info("processing account {} card {}
         balance={}", id, pan, amount), the appender masks the offending
         substrings before the line is emitted.

      2. application-test.properties: pins Hibernate SQL parameter
         loggers at WARN so JDBC bound parameters never reach the layout.

      3. LoggingPiiRedactionTest (AAP §0.10.5): a dedicated JUnit 5 test
         class that drives selected service paths with known-PII fixtures
         and asserts via a ListAppender that captured log lines do NOT
         contain account numbers, card numbers, or balances. THIS FILE IS
         NECESSARY BUT NOT SUFFICIENT; the assertion test is the ultimate
         compliance gate.

    REDACTION ORDER (longest patterns first; innermost executes first)
    Logback's nested %replace calls execute from innermost to outermost
    on the raw %m (message) text:

        innermost : card numbers (13 to 19 digits, PAN range)
        middle    : account IDs  (11 digits, CardDemo CVACT01Y width)
        outermost : balances     (labelled decimal amounts, PIC V99 scale 2)

    Longest-first ordering matters: an 11-digit subsequence inside a
    16-digit PAN must NOT be mis-masked as an account number. By
    replacing the PAN first with a non-numeric token
    (****CARD-MASKED****), the account regex sees no residual digits
    from the card and cannot accidentally partial-match.

    MINIMAL CHANGE CLAUSE (AAP §0.10.2)
    Only the appenders, layouts, and filters required for the test
    pyramid are configured here. No file appender (test logs are
    ephemeral; Surefire captures stdout to target/surefire-reports/),
    no async appender (synchronous output is fine for the wall-clock
    targets in AAP §0.7.2), no rolling policy, no SMTP/email appender,
    no plaintext credentials. Package-specific log levels for the
    application (e.g., logging.level.com.aws.carddemo=DEBUG) are driven
    from application-test.properties via Spring Boot's
    LogbackLoggingSystem; they are deliberately NOT hardcoded here so
    the properties file remains the single source of truth.
    ============================================================================
-->
<configuration scan="false" debug="false">

    <!--
        Include Spring Boot's standard Logback defaults. Exposes the
        property names LOG_LEVEL_PATTERN, LOG_DATEFORMAT_PATTERN, PID,
        CONSOLE_LOG_CHARSET, etc., and registers Spring Boot's converter
        words (clr, wEx, applicationName, correlationId). Importing the
        defaults keeps this test file aligned with the framework's
        expectations and means future Spring Boot upgrades that adjust
        the default format are picked up automatically.
    -->
    <include resource="org/springframework/boot/logging/logback/defaults.xml"/>

    <!--
        ========================================================================
        PCI / PII redaction regex properties (AAP §0.10.5)
        ========================================================================
        These regex / mask pairs are referenced inside the CONSOLE appender's
        pattern via Logback's %replace conversion word. Defining them as
        Logback properties keeps the appender pattern legible and lets any
        future appender (e.g., JSON-encoded structured logs) reuse the same
        redaction policy without duplicating the regular expressions.

        Regex notes
          * Single backslash escape; Logback's %replace passes the regex
            string verbatim to java.util.regex.Pattern#compile, so `\b`
            (word boundary) and `\d` (digit) are written with a single
            backslash in the XML, NOT doubled.
          * Anchored on word boundaries (`\b`) to avoid partial matches
            inside longer digit runs and to allow the regex engine to skip
            quickly through non-digit text.
          * No `.*` or look-arounds; keeps each match O(n) in message
            length with no risk of catastrophic backtracking.
    -->

    <!-- Card numbers (PAN): 13 to 19 consecutive digits.
         Covers Visa (13 or 16), Mastercard (16), Amex (15), Discover
         (16), Maestro (12 to 19; lower bound deliberately conservative
         at 13 to avoid mis-masking 12-digit timestamps or counts). -->
    <property name="CARD_NUMBER_REGEX" value="\b\d{13,19}\b"/>
    <property name="CARD_NUMBER_MASK"  value="****CARD-MASKED****"/>

    <!-- Account numbers: exactly 11 consecutive digits.
         Matches the COBOL CVACT01Y.cpy account-id width (PIC X(11)).
         CardDemo test fixtures use IDs such as "00000000010" through
         "00000000060". The card regex above runs first, so any 16-digit
         PAN has already been masked to a non-numeric token by the time
         this pattern is applied. -->
    <property name="ACCOUNT_NUMBER_REGEX" value="\b\d{11}\b"/>
    <property name="ACCOUNT_NUMBER_MASK"  value="****ACCT-MASKED****"/>

    <!-- Balance / amount: a label ("balance" or "amount",
         case-insensitive) followed by an optional `:` or `=` separator
         and a signed decimal value with exactly TWO fractional digits;
         i.e., the COBOL PIC V99 scale=2 monetary convention.
         The leading label anchor is deliberate: it avoids masking
         non-monetary decimals such as response times, page sizes, or
         coverage percentages while still catching every
         business-amount log call that follows the project's
         "label=value" convention. Capture group $1 preserves the
         original label casing in the redacted output. -->
    <property name="BALANCE_REGEX" value="(?i)(balance|amount)\s*[:=]?\s*[-]?\d+\.\d{2}"/>
    <property name="BALANCE_MASK"  value="$1=****AMT-MASKED****"/>

    <!--
        ========================================================================
        CONSOLE appender, defence-in-depth PII redaction
        ========================================================================
        Synchronous ConsoleAppender with a PatternLayoutEncoder. The
        pattern chains three %replace conversion words; nesting is
        right-to-left, so the INNERMOST %replace operates on the raw
        %m message first (card numbers), then the result feeds the
        middle %replace (account numbers), and finally the outermost
        %replace (balances).

        Layout breakdown:
          %d{...}                : ISO-8601 timestamp (Spring Boot default)
          ${LOG_LEVEL_PATTERN}   : 5-char right-padded level (e.g., "INFO ")
          [%t]                   : thread name (useful for parallel JUnit 5
                                   runs where multiple worker threads
                                   interleave their output)
          %-40.40logger{39}      : logger name, abbreviated to 39 chars,
                                   padded/truncated to width 40
          : %replace(...)        : separator and the chained masking pipeline
          %n                     : platform-specific line separator
    -->
    <appender name="CONSOLE" class="ch.qos.logback.core.ConsoleAppender">
        <encoder class="ch.qos.logback.classic.encoder.PatternLayoutEncoder">
            <pattern>%d{${LOG_DATEFORMAT_PATTERN:-yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm:ss.SSS}} ${LOG_LEVEL_PATTERN:-%5p} [%t] %-40.40logger{39} : %replace(%replace(%replace(%m){'${CARD_NUMBER_REGEX}','${CARD_NUMBER_MASK}'}){'${ACCOUNT_NUMBER_REGEX}','${ACCOUNT_NUMBER_MASK}'}){'${BALANCE_REGEX}','${BALANCE_MASK}'}%n</pattern>
            <charset>${CONSOLE_LOG_CHARSET:-UTF-8}</charset>
        </encoder>
    </appender>

    <!--
        ========================================================================
        Verbose third-party libraries: noise reduction + defence in depth
        ========================================================================
        Pinning Hibernate SQL loggers at WARN serves two purposes:
          1. Keeps test stdout focused on the application's own log
             output (Hibernate at DEBUG emits one line per SQL statement
             plus one line per bound parameter, overwhelming on a batch
             IT that posts thousands of transactions).
          2. PII defence in depth: JDBC parameter binding at TRACE
             would echo balance and account-id values straight to the
             layout, which the %replace chain would then have to mask.
             Suppressing the source eliminates that risk entirely.

        Testcontainers and Docker-Java are kept at the levels that
        balance diagnosability against noise: container-startup
        progress is helpful when Docker has trouble; the Docker REST
        API chatter is not.

        Application-level loggers (com.aws.carddemo.*) are deliberately
        NOT declared here; application-test.properties owns those
        levels via Spring Boot's LogbackLoggingSystem, per AAP §0.10.2.
    -->
    <logger name="org.hibernate.SQL"                               level="WARN"/>
    <logger name="org.hibernate.type.descriptor.sql"               level="WARN"/>
    <logger name="org.hibernate.orm.jdbc.bind"                     level="WARN"/>
    <logger name="org.hibernate.engine.jdbc.spi.SqlStatementLogger" level="WARN"/>
    <logger name="com.github.dockerjava"                           level="WARN"/>
    <logger name="org.testcontainers"                              level="INFO"/>

    <!--
        ========================================================================
        Root logger
        ========================================================================
        Default to INFO; package-specific overrides
        (logging.level.com.aws.carddemo=DEBUG, etc.) are applied by
        Spring Boot from application-test.properties after Logback
        bootstrap completes. The CONSOLE appender is the only
        attached output; file appenders are intentionally omitted
        (see Minimal Change Clause notes in the header comment).
    -->
    <root level="INFO">
        <appender-ref ref="CONSOLE"/>
    </root>

</configuration>
